PHASE 1: Why Spark? (Very Important for Interviews)

Learn These Concepts First (Before Coding)

You must be able to explain these verbally:

🔹 Why Not Pandas?

Pandas	Spark

Single machine	Distributed
Memory-bound	Scales across cluster
Fast for small data	Fast for big data


Interview line:

> “Pandas works in-memory on a single machine, whereas Spark distributes data across nodes and processes it in parallel.”




---

🔹 What Problems Spark Solves

Big data (TBs, PBs)

Slow MapReduce

Fault tolerance

Scalability



---

PHASE 2: Spark Architecture (CRITICAL)

This is the most important interview topic.

🧠 Core Architecture Components

You must know:

1️⃣ Driver

Runs your main program

Creates SparkSession

Builds execution plan

Coordinates executors


2️⃣ Executors

Run tasks

Store data in memory

Exist on worker nodes


3️⃣ Cluster Manager

YARN / Kubernetes / Standalone

Allocates resources


4️⃣ Jobs → Stages → Tasks

Job: Triggered by an action

Stage: Group of tasks

Task: Smallest unit of work


📌 Interview sentence:

> “A Spark job is divided into stages based on shuffles, and stages consist of parallel tasks executed by executors.”




---

🔹 Spark Execution Flow (Memorize This)

1. Write PySpark code


2. Transformations build DAG


3. Action triggers execution


4. DAG → stages → tasks


5. Executors execute tasks




---

PHASE 3: Core Spark Concepts (Must-Know)

🔹 Lazy Evaluation

Transformations do not execute immediately

Execution happens only when an action is called


Examples:

select(), filter() → lazy

show(), count() → action


📌 Interview answer:

> “Spark uses lazy evaluation to optimize execution by building a DAG before running jobs.”




---

🔹 Transformations vs Actions

Transformations	Actions

select	show
filter	count
withColumn	collect



---

🔹 DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph)

Logical execution plan

Optimized by Catalyst Optimizer



---

PHASE 4: PySpark Basics (Hands-On Core)

Now you start actual PySpark.

🔹 SparkSession

from pyspark.sql import SparkSession

spark = SparkSession.builder \
    .appName("InterviewPrep") \
    .getOrCreate()

📌 Interview:

> “SparkSession is the entry point for DataFrame and SQL APIs.”




---

🔹 DataFrames (MOST IMPORTANT API)

You must know:

read

select

filter

withColumn

groupBy

agg

orderBy


Example:

df = spark.read.csv("data.csv", header=True, inferSchema=True)

df.select("name", "salary") \
  .filter(df.salary > 50000) \
  .groupBy("department") \
  .count()


---

🔹 DataFrame vs RDD

RDD	DataFrame

Low-level	High-level
Manual optimization	Optimized
No schema	Schema-based


📌 Interview answer:

> “RDDs are low-level and give more control, but DataFrames are preferred due to Catalyst optimization.”




---

PHASE 5: Spark SQL (VERY IMPORTANT)

Spark SQL makes PySpark interview-friendly.

df.createOrReplaceTempView("employees")

spark.sql("""
SELECT department, COUNT(*)
FROM employees
WHERE salary > 50000
GROUP BY department
""")

📌 Interview line:

> “Spark SQL allows us to run SQL queries on distributed data using Catalyst optimizer.”




---

PHASE 6: Performance Concepts (Interview Gold)

You don’t need deep tuning—just concepts.

🔹 Partitioning

Data split across executors

Too few → underutilization

Too many → overhead


df.repartition(10)
df.coalesce(5)


---

🔹 Shuffle

Expensive operation

Happens in groupBy, join, orderBy


📌 Interview:

> “Shuffle redistributes data across nodes and is expensive.”




---

🔹 Caching

df.cache()
df.persist()

Why?

Reuse intermediate results

Speeds up iterative jobs



